{"causes": ["The competitive aristocratic culture of Archaic Athens fostered a strong emphasis on athletic achievement as a means of demonstrating wealth and social standing.", "The establishment and prominence of the ancient Olympic Games provided a premier international platform for wealthy individuals to display their prowess and gain renown.", "The political instability and intermittent tyranny in Athens under figures like Peisistratos created a context where strategic alliances and concessions were necessary for aristocratic survival and influence."], "effects": ["Cimon's Olympic victories significantly elevated his family's prestige, paving the way for his son Miltiades the Younger to achieve prominence.", "His strategic concession of an Olympic victory to Peisistratos secured his own return from exile and cemented his family's political position within Athens.", "Cimon's athletic legacy contributed to the enduring cultural value placed on competitive excellence and physical prowess in Classical Athenian society."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "cimon-coalemos", "sourceName": "Cimon Coalemos", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "miltiades-the-younger", "targetName": "Miltiades the Younger", "context": "Cimon's established family prestige and political maneuvering directly enabled the rise of his son, Miltiades the Younger, to become a prominent Athenian general."}, {"sourceSlug": "olympic-games-ancient", "sourceName": "Ancient Olympic Games", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "chariot-racing", "targetName": "Chariot Racing", "context": "The Tethrippon, a four-horse chariot race, was a premier event within the Ancient Olympic Games in which Cimon excelled."}, {"sourceSlug": "cimon-coalemos", "sourceName": "Cimon Coalemos", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "olympic-games-ancient", "targetName": "Ancient Olympic Games", "context": "Cimon Coalemos famously participated in and won the four-horse chariot race three times at the Ancient Olympic Games between 572 and 564 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "peisistratos", "sourceName": "Peisistratos", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "cimon-coalemos", "targetName": "Cimon Coalemos", "context": "The political power of Peisistratos directly influenced Cimon's decision to concede an Olympic victory to secure his return from exile in 564 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "athens-greece", "sourceName": "Athens, Greece", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "cimon-coalemos", "targetName": "Cimon Coalemos", "context": "Cimon Coalemos was a prominent aristocratic figure and athlete whose political actions and family legacy were centered in Athens during the 6th century BCE."}], "places": [{"name": "Athens, Greece", "role": "Homeland, Political Center"}, {"name": "Olympia, Greece", "role": "Site of Victories"}, {"name": "Peloponnese, Greece", "role": "Region of Games"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Cimon Coalemos", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "cimon-coalemos"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q14278582", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cimon_Coalemos"], "tags": ["Athletes & Competitors", "cause-and-effect", "cultural-diffusion", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Cimon Coalemos's unprecedented Olympic victories and strategic political maneuvering significantly enhanced his family's standing, directly enabling the later prominence of his son, Miltiades the Younger, a pivotal figure in the Persian Wars. His actions exemplify the intertwining of aristocratic athletic achievement and political power in Archaic Greece, leaving a clear legacy for the subsequent Athenian golden age.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-25T07:02:52Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40168899637", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Cimon Coalemos (b. 600 BCE, Athens) was a athlete or sports figure of the Classical era, an age of empires, philosophical inquiry, and foundational civilizations, active in Athens. Known in the fields of athletes & competitors.", "newValue": "Cimon Coalemos, an aristocratic Athenian athlete, established a notable lineage of political and military leaders through his exceptional success in the ancient Olympic Games during the Archaic period, specifically between 600 and 560 BCE. His three consecutive victories in the prestigious four-horse chariot race, known as the Tethrippon, not only brought immense personal glory but also solidified his family's prestige within the Athenian social and political landscape. This era, preceding the Persian Wars, saw the burgeoning of Athenian democracy alongside a powerful aristocracy, where athletic prowess was a direct indicator of wealth, divine favor, and social standing, crucial for political influence.

His athletic career culminated in three successive Olympic triumphs, a feat so rare that only one other individual, the Spartan Euagoras, achieved it. Cimon's first two victories, likely in 572 and 568 BCE, were publicly attributed to him. However, his third victory in 564 BCE saw a remarkable act of political maneuvering: Cimon publicly announced that he was ceding the honor of this third win to Peisistratos, the powerful tyrant who had recently returned to power in Athens. This gesture was a calculated move to facilitate Cimon's return from exile, demonstrating the intricate connection between athletic glory, personal liberty, and political power in Archaic Greece.

Cimon Coalemos's legacy extended far beyond his individual athletic achievements. His strategic concession to Peisistratos secured his family's place and wealth in Athens, directly enabling the rise of his son, Miltiades the Younger, who would later become the celebrated general credited with the Athenian victory at the Battle of Marathon in 490 BCE. His athletic fame also contributed to the cultural emphasis on physical excellence and competitive spirit that characterized classical Athenian society, influencing its martial traditions and civic ideals."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-25T07:02:52Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40168899637", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "600 BCE, Athens"}, {"title": "Development of competitive athletics and sporting traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "600 BCE, Athens"}], "newValue": ["The competitive aristocratic culture of Archaic Athens fostered a strong emphasis on athletic achievement as a means of demonstrating wealth and social standing.", "The establishment and prominence of the ancient Olympic Games provided a premier international platform for wealthy individuals to display their prowess and gain renown.", "The political instability and intermittent tyranny in Athens under figures like Peisistratos created a context where strategic alliances and concessions were necessary for aristocratic survival and influence."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-25T07:02:52Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40168899637", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Inspired sporting achievement and athletic culture", "type": "Idea", "year": "560 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "560 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Cimon's Olympic victories significantly elevated his family